"city":"Toronto","country":"Canada"},"grass":false,"images":[{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/nhl/day/1838.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day"]}]},"attendance":19253,"officials":[{"fullName":"TJ Luxmore","displayName":"TJ Luxmore","position":{"name":"Referee","displayName":"Referee","id":"24"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Tom Chmielewski","displayName":"Tom Chmielewski","position":{"name":"Referee","displayName":"Referee","id":"24"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Shandor Alphonso","displayName":"Shandor Alphonso","position":{"name":"Linesman","displayName":"Linesman","id":"25"},"order":0},{"fullName":"Kyle Flemington","displayName":"Kyle Flemington","position":{"name":"Linesman","displayName":"Linesman","id":"25"},"order":0}]},"article":{"headline":"Newhook scores twice in the Canadiens’ 4-2 win over the Maple Leafs","description":"— Alex Newhook scored twice in the first period, Jacob Fowler made 26 saves and the Montreal Canadiens beat the Toronto Maple Leafs 4-2 on Saturday night.","story":"TORONTO -- — <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/4565237/alex-newhook\">Alex Newhook</a> scored twice in the first period, <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5188437/jacob-fowler\">Jacob Fowler</a> made 26 saves and the <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/team/_/name/mtl/montreal-canadiens\">Montreal Canadiens</a> beat the <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/team/_/name/tor/toronto-maple-leafs\">Toronto Maple Leafs</a> 4-2 on Saturday night.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5833/chris-kreider\">Chris Kreider</a> and Zachary Bolduc also scored for Montreal (11-3-3). The Canadiens have won seven of their last eight games.\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/3114736/william-nylander\">William Nylander</a> and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5342529/gavin-mckenna\">Gavin McKenna</a> scored for Toronto (8-8-2), and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/nhl/player/_/id/5571/sergei-bobrovsky\">Sergei Bobrovsky</a> stopped 24 shots. The Maple Leafs have lost two straight and four of five in regulation.\n\n\r\n              The Canadiens opened the scoring at 5:56 of the first when Newhook’s pass in front of Bobrovsky pinballed into Toronto’s net after a giveaway by McKenna started the sequence. Newhook then tipped in Kaiden Guhle’s point shot with 6:54 left.\n\n\r\n              The Canadiens made it 3-0 on a breakaway with 7:03 to go in the second when Kreider intercepted McKenna's pass at Montreal’s blue line and beat Bobrovsky past his blocker. Toronto’s rookie winger was benched for the rest of the period.\n\n\r\n              Nylander blasted a one-timer on a power play at 6:19 of the third to get the Maple Leafs on the board before McKenna popped in a loose puck 55 seconds later, but Bolduc responded on a man advantage to make it 4-2 with 7:45 to go.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Up next</hl2>\r\n              Canadiens: Host Minnesota on Tuesday night.\n\n\r\n              Maple Leafs: Host New Jersey on Sunday night.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              \r\n                <a href=\"https://apnews.com/hub/nhl\">See AP’s full NHL coverage here</a>","published":"2026-11-08T03:01:34Z"}}